From: Folio <folio@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] border-radius: percentages and elliptical corners

CSS Backgrounds 3 gives every corner a horizontal and a vertical radius, and lets either be a
percentage of the border box: `border-radius: 50%` on a box that is not square is an ellipse, and
`10px 20px / 5px` gives the vertical radii separately. RmlUi takes one length per corner, so a
percentage fails to parse and the corner is square, and there is no way to write an ellipse.

Each corner's radius is now two longhands, `border-*-radius-x` and `-y`, the way RmlUi already
splits `transform-origin`. `border-*-radius` becomes a shorthand taking one or two values, and
`border-radius` a shorthand of its own type that reads the slash. Percentages are kept unresolved
in the computed values and resolved against each box when its geometry is generated, horizontal
against the width and vertical against the height. Overlapping radii are scaled by one factor per
CSS 5.5, horizontal radii against the width and vertical against the height. Box shadows take
their radii from each box rather than the element, since a percentage resolves differently per box.

RenderBox::GetBorderRadius now returns CornerSizes2, so code drawing its own shape from it sees
both radii. A corner with either radius zero is square, as in CSS.

Removable once upstream supports percentage and elliptical radii.

